<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Xiaozong of Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1127">1127</a>–<a href="/years/1194">1194</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Zhao Shen, Xiaozong, Zhao Bocong, Zhao Yuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Xiaozong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, personal name Zhao Shen, was the 11th emperor of the Song dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the second of the Southern Song, a seventh-generation descendant of its founder, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-song">Taizu</a>. Adopted by Emperor Gaozong in 1160, he came to the throne in 1162 when Gaozong abdicated, although the retired emperor remained the de facto ruler until his death in 1187. Xiaozong rehabilitated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> posthumously, made peace with the Jin dynasty in 1165, kept close control over his ministers and worked to stabilise the paper currency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huizi">huizi</a>, whose value was steadied by 1175. He abdicated in 1189 in favour of his son, Emperor Guangzong, and died in 1194.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaozong-of-song.jpg" alt="Emperor Xiaozong of Song" width="330" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:宋孝宗皇帝.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhao Shen was born Zhao Bocong, the second son of Zhao Zicheng, a sixth cousin of Emperor Gaozong. After the Jingkang Incident of 1127 Gaozong's father, eldest brother and most close relatives were captured by the Jin. Gaozong's only son, Zhao Fu, had died at about the age of two, so the emperor had his officials search for living descendants of the imperial family. Zhao Shen was found and adopted in April 1160, along with another relative, Zhao Qu.</p><p>Gaozong chose Shen over Qu as heir, it is said, because of his virtue: given ten maids, Qu touched every one and Shen not one. He was named crown prince in July 1162.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Gaozong abdicated on 24 July 1162 and kept power as Taishang Huang, so Xiaozong took full power only after Gaozong's death in 1187. He reached peace with the Jin in 1165, rehabilitated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> after death and cleared out the remnants of Qin Hui's faction. Under him trade missions to the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> increased.</p><p>His was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> reigns not dominated by powerful officials. He dismissed ministers who did not meet his expectations, and in 1166 an official, Mo Chi, criticised him for removing councillors after a few months; he praised the criticism but ignored it. He issued direct orders, called mi-pai, nei-pi and yu-pi, without consulting his councillors.</p><p>Xiaozong drove attempts to steady the paper currency, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huizi">huizi</a>. In 1166 he bought back two million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ounces' worth of circulating notes and burned them, and decreed that paper notes could pay taxes and fees. By limiting the quantity in circulation he stabilised the value in 1175; around 20 million strings of cash were in issue in 1186.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Gaozong died in 1187 Xiaozong withdrew into mourning and handed government to his son Zhao Dun. In 1189 he abdicated in his favour as Emperor Guangzong and became Taishang Huang. He fell ill in 1194, and Guangzong refused to visit him or attend the funeral, which forced Guangzong to give up the throne to Xiaozong's grandson, Emperor Ningzong. He is remembered as an effective ruler under whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> prospered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Xiaozong of Song” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiu Prefecture, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.73,120.73&amp;year=1170" aria-label="Xiu Prefecture on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emperor Xiaozong of Song"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 4937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 4937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.73,120.73&amp;year=1170">What was here in 1170? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
